// Row displacement packs the rows of the action table into one shared array, so a state's entries end up interleaved
// with the entries of other states. What keeps a lookup honest is the parallel check array, which records the column a
// cell was written for: a state reading a cell which belongs to another state finds a column which is not the one it
// asked for and falls through to its default action instead. A driver which omits that comparison, or which compares
// against the wrong thing, still parses a grammar whose rows do not overlap, and goes wrong here.
//
// The grammar is shaped to produce that overlap. It has many terminals, which makes the rows wide, and every keyword
// leads to a state which accepts exactly one or two of them, which makes the rows nearly empty. Placing rows that sparse
// leaves each of them sitting inside the span of several others.
//
// It also produces the other half of the scheme. The states which follow "echo", "foxtrot" and "golf" accept the same
// two terminals and shift them to the same states, so their action rows are identical and share a single displacement
// rather than being placed twice. Three states reading the very same cells is exactly the situation the check array has
// to survive. Their goto rows are not identical, because each of them continues into a different statement, so the two
// tables place these states differently and a driver which confuses the two displacements goes wrong here.
//
// Note that a displacement is never negative here, because the placement clamps it at zero, so no driver has to index
// its arrays with a negative offset.
//
// The input uses every keyword once, so that every one of those rows is actually read.
//
// The scanner is not what this case is about, but its rules do have to put the keywords before the general name rule,
// or the keywords would never be matched.

@scanner {
    WHITESPACE: /[ \t\n]+/ @skip;

    ALPHA:   "alpha";
    BRAVO:   "bravo";
    CHARLIE: "charlie";
    DELTA:   "delta";
    ECHO:    "echo";
    FOXTROT: "foxtrot";
    GOLF:    "golf";

    NUMBER:  /[0-9]+/;
    NAME:    /[a-z]+/;

    SEMICOLON: ";";
}

@parser {
    program
        : program statement
        | statement
        ;

    statement
        : "alpha"   NUMBER ";"
        | "bravo"   NAME ";"
        | "charlie" NUMBER ";"
        | "delta"   NAME ";"
        | "echo"    value ";"
        | "foxtrot" value ";"
        | "golf"    value ";"
        ;

    value
        : NUMBER
        | NAME
        ;
}
